ing B7 might be reasonable to reduce suit spread.  \n\nHowever, I have a J3 meld, so aiming for \"All Types\" (6 points) requires having all three suits and honors. I already have honors (J3), and have W, T, B suits in hand, so keeping all three suits is beneficial for that pattern. Discarding B7 would still leave me with B2, B4, B6, so I keep B suit.  \n\nGiven the isolated nature of W9 and the fact that it's a terminal with no immediate pairing or sequence, discarding W9 seems best to move towards a more balanced hand without losing potential chows in T and B suits.  \n\nReason: W9 is a terminal tile with no supporting tiles in hand (only other W tiles are W1, W6, W7 which don't connect well with W9). Discarding it helps reduce the number of isolated terminals and keeps the T2-T3-T4 sequence intact and B tiles for potential chows. It is also relatively safe based on discard history (W9 not heavily discarded, but terminals often have lower risk of giving away pungs since many are already out).  \n\nAnswer: Play W9"}
